  "analysis": "Root is fuse-overlayfs backed by /dev/vdb which reports 984G/956G used, 0 avail (100%). apt-get update succeeded writing ~31MB to /var/lib/apt/lists, but the gcc install pre-flight failed wanting 71MB+221MB. There's only one >20MB file in the container's view: a large apt Packages index. The cgroup tmpfs has 32G (but is noexec) and /dev/shm is 64M (noexec). I need to determine whether real writes beyond ~31MB are possible, or whether I must operate in RAM. Let me test actual write capacity and inspect the apt lists size.",
  "plan": "1) Write a 200MB file to /app to test whether the writable layer can actually accept more data (df may be misleading). 2) Check size of /var/lib/apt/lists to find space to reclaim. 3) Check whether /dev/shm can be remounted exec so RAM could host a toolchain. Based on results I'll either free space + apt install gcc, or pivot to a RAM-based toolchain.",
